110:
Sumedha the hermit said to the people, "Friends, if you are clearing this path for the Buddha, please give me a portion as well. I too will clear the path for the Buddha along with you." Then, those people, accepting his request saying "Very well," and knowing that "This wise Sumedha is possessed of great psychic power and majesty," marked out a portion that was difficult to clear, mixed with water, and extremely uneven, and gave it to him saying, "Please clear and decorate this spot."

Then, the wise Sumedha, generating joy with the Buddha as the object, thought: "I am able to make this spot exceedingly beautiful by means of psychic power. However, doing so would not satisfy me. Today, it is proper for me to perform service through physical labor." Thus thinking, he brought earth and filled that place.

While Sumedha the hermit had not yet finished clearing that spot, the people of the city of Rammavatī announced the time to the Blessed One, saying, "Venerable Sir, the meal is ready." When the time was thus announced by the people, the Buddha, the Possessor of Ten Powers, dressed himself by properly covering the three circles with a double-layered robe that was the color of the Jayasumana flower. Over that undergarment, he fastened a waistband as glorious as a streak of lightning, as if encircling a bundle of Jayasumana flowers with a golden band. He donned a magnificent red rag-robe (paṃsukūla), the color of a Bandhujīvaka flower soaked in lac-dye, appearing like lac-dye being poured over the peak of a golden mountain, or like a golden pagoda surrounded by a net of coral, or like a golden shrine covered with a red blanket, or like the autumnal moon covered by a red cloud. He emerged from the door of the Fragrant Chamber (Gandhakuṭi) like a lion emerging from a golden cave and stood at the portico of the Fragrant Chamber. Then, all the monks took their own bowls and robes and surrounded the Blessed One. The monks who stood surrounding him were of such nature:

They had few desires and were contented; they were admonishers and patient of the words of others;
They dwelt in seclusion and were unmingled with the laity; they were well-disciplined and censurers of evil.

All those monks were perfect in virtue and skilled in concentration and jhāna;
They were perfect in wisdom and liberation, and endowed with the fifteen conducts (caraṇa).

They were cankerless (khīṇāsava) and had attained mastery; they were powerful and famous;
They had peaceful faculties and had attained self-control; they were pure and had ended rebirth.

210:
In those verses, "uttariṃ vatamadhiṭṭhāsi" means that he made an even firmer effort to fulfill the perfections (pāramīs).

The capital city of that Blessed Anomadassī was named Candavatī. His father was King Yasavā, and his mother was Yasodharā. Nisabha and Anoma were the two chief disciples. The attendant was Varuṇa. Sundarī and Sumanā were the two chief female disciples. The Bodhi tree was the Ajjuna tree. His height was fifty-eight cubits. His lifespan was one hundred thousand years. Sirimā was the chief queen, and his son was Upavāṇa. He lived in the household life for ten thousand years. The Bodhisatta renounced the world by a palanquin. His departure by palanquin should be understood in the same manner as the departure by palace mentioned in the commentary on the Chronicle of Sobhita Buddha. King Dhammaka was his attendant. It is said that the Blessed One resided in the Dhammārāma monastery. Therefore, the Blessed One said:

"The city was named Candavatī, the king was named Yasavā;
The mother of the Teacher Anomadassī was named Yasodharā.
Nisabha and Anoma were the chief disciples;
The attendant of the Teacher Anomadassī was named Varuṇa.
Sundarī and Sumanā were the chief female disciples;
The Bodhi tree of that Blessed One is called the Ajjuna.
The Great Sage was fifty-eight cubits in height;
His radiance streamed forth like the rising sun with a hundred rays.
At that time, the lifespan was one hundred thousand years;
Remaining for that long, he saved many people.
With Arahants of such quality, the Word of the Buddha was in full bloom;
With Arahants free from passion and stainless, the Dispensation of the Conqueror was beautiful.
That Teacher of immeasurable fame and those incomparable pairs [of disciples];
All that has disappeared—are not all formations indeed empty?"

"Pabhā niddhāvatī" means that rays of light emitted from the body of that Blessed Anomadassī. That radiance constantly spread and remained over an area of twelve yojanas. "Yugāni tāni" refers to the pairs such as the chief disciples. "Sabbaṃ tamantarahitaṃ" means that all the things mentioned have entered the state of impermanence and perished. There is also a variant reading "nanu rittakāyeva saṅkhārā." It means, "Are not all formations indeed empty, void, and without essence?" The letter "ma" is inserted for euphonic junction (padasandhi). The meaning of the remaining verses is clear.

It was in the presence of this Blessed Anomadassī that the Venerable Sāriputta and the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna made their aspiration to become chief disciples. Although the story of these elders should be told here, it should be understood that I have not included it for fear of making the text too long.
